//? Title: PageRank: actual random-surfer mass transfers
//? Subtitle: Uniform teleportation, dangling pages and finite iteration status
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Derive uniform-teleport PageRank, inspect one actual update with a dangling
page, and retain the original three-page ranking. Canonical before/after vectors
and edge-transfer histories show exactly which probability mass moves, while
explicit convergence status separates a stopped iteration from a finite-budget
approximation.
)

\toc

> The stochastic link model

For n>0, a source chooses uniformly among its outgoing edge occurrences.
Repeated edges therefore count with multiplicity; self-loops are allowed. A
dangling source with no outgoing edge chooses uniformly among all n pages. Let P
be this row-stochastic transition matrix. With finite damping0≤d<1, the
random-surfer map is

$$ F(r)=\frac{1-d}{n}\mathbf1+dP^\top r, \qquad r^*=F(r^*). $$
\alttext(The next rank vector combines uniform teleportation with damped stochastic link transfers; the stationary rank vector is unchanged by that map.)

In exact arithmetic, nonnegative unit mass remains unit mass. Since P is
row-stochastic, its transpose is nonexpansive in the L1 norm. Consequently

$$ \lVert F(x)-F(y)\rVert_1\le d\lVert x-y\rVert_1. $$
\alttext(One random-surfer update contracts the L1 distance between rank vectors by at most the damping factor.)

For d<1 this contraction gives a unique fixed point and geometric convergence.
The implementation evaluates a serial sparse update in double precision, from a
uniform initial vector. It stops strictly when the actual L1 state change is
below the tolerance, or returns its budgeted iterate with converged=false. The
same exact-model contraction yields an a posteriori bound d/(1−d) times the last
state change for the returned iterate; this diagnostic excludes floating
arithmetic error and is not a certified enclosure.

> One actual update with a dangling page

Use two pages and the single edge0→1. From initial[.5,.5], page1 contributes.5
dangling mass. At d=.85 the uniform base is.2875 per destination, while the
actual edge adds.425 to page1. The returned first iterate is[.2875,.7125]. Both
the diagram and table read actual producer observations, including the committed
destination addition; diagram states are rounded to four decimals for display.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/page_rank_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/page_rank.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::page_rank_trace(2, {{0, 1}}, {.85, 1e-12, 1, 1000});
    auto &s = t.iterations[0];
    auto &e = t.transfers[0];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("base", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual dangling mass {}\nuniform base {}",
                       s.dangling_mass, s.uniform_base));
    d.node("edge", {8, 0},
           std::format("Actual edge {} to {}\ncontribution {}", e.source,
                       e.destination, e.contribution));
    d.node("after", {16, 0},
           std::format("Actual first iterate\n[{:.4f}, {:.4f}]", s.after[0],
                       s.after[1]));
    d.edge("base", "after").edge("edge", "after");
    std::print("Actual old vector: `{}`. Actual new vector: `{}`. Actual change: {}. Converged: {}.\n\n",
               s.before, s.after, s.l1_change, t.run.converged);
    std::print("| actual source | actual destination | old source rank | actual contribution | destination " "before | destination after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &e : t.transfers)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", e.source, e.destination,
                   e.source_rank, e.contribution, e.destination_before,
                   e.destination_after);
    d.save_svg("build/page_rank_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
